{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "321",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 60s presented with an itchy plaque on the mid-back. She had had a lesion at that site since childhood, which had remained asymptomatic and pea sized for most of that time. However, over the 10 years prior to presentation it had gradually increased in size and became itchy. There was also occasional oozing of serosanguineous fluid and bleeding from the lesion.\n\nExamination revealed a 1.5 × 1.0-cm well-defined erythematous plaque with overlying ulceration on the mid-back, 1 cm left of the spine. Adjoining skin showed a horseshoe-shaped oval area of lichenification.\n\n- Histopathological examination:    - Revealed a circumscribed tumor contiguous with adjoining normal epidermis and delineated from underlying dermis by continuous apocrine epithelial lining    - Tumor epithelium with multiple papillary projections with vascular, plasma cell–rich stroma    - Several foci showed papillary projections lined by multilayered epithelium with atypical cells, few mitotic figures, and areas of necrosis but no dermal invasion      - Immunohistochemical analysis:    - p63 staining positive in myoepithelial cell layers    - Adjacent skin showed dermal papillae with congophilic amorphous eosinophilic deposits    - Pan-cytokeratin (AE1/3) immunostain positive  Images:  Figure 1 Title: Clinical Image of the Patient  Description: A 1.5 × 1.0-cm well-defined erythematous plaque with indented margin and overlying ulceration, located on the mid-back 1 cm left to the spine. Adjoining skin shows a horseshoe-shaped oval area of lichenification.  Figure 2 Title: Histopathologic Findings From Lesional Specimens  Description: Multiple microscopic images showing tumor architecture, cellular details, immunohistochemical staining, and dermal deposits.",
